like pulling together all of
the previous conversations

00:11:25.083 --> 00:11:28.323
that you just mentioned that
we had had over the years,

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:32.973
it very quickly gained momentum that yeah,

00:11:32.973 --> 00:11:37.973
this can be about us as kids, basically.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:43.550
And then we started to pitch
that and it gained momentum.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.673
And it very, very quickly,
I'd say almost immediately,

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.900
we knew that we weren't
going to make another

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:50.880
version of the Hardy Boys.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.486
I was a big Hardy Boys fan growing up.

00:11:53.486 --> 00:11:56.240
A big choose your own
adventure fan as well.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:57.960
But that's for younger kids.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:00.950
It's younger kids, and
there's a campy-ness to it.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:02.590
And this is, we were like, we wanna make

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:04.770
a book that's got humor in it, of course,

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:09.770
but we want this to be sincerely
dramatic, and thrilling,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:15.220
and scary, and dark, along
with that humor, and also--

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:18.990
And for us, and in terms
of target audience,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.227
something that we would want to read.

00:12:21.227 --> 00:12:23.260
This is not a children's book.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:24.230
No.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.460
There's been lots of talk of
like is this an adult book,

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:28.900
is this a young adult book?

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:31.770
All we can say is that
it's a lot like what we do

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:32.683
with everything else we do,

00:12:32.683 --> 00:12:34.470
it's kind of for everybody.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.810
I mean sure, I mean like Shepherd asked me

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.220
can I read it, I was like yeah,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.910
it's got some stuff in there
that is a little off color

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.250
and might scare you a little bit, but,

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.330
you know, he can take it, and then anybody

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:48.720
who is in high school right now, I mean,

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.460
because these characters
are going into high school,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.410
it makes sense for them to read it.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.850
And of course, if you're like
us and you grew up in the 90s,

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.910
there's a lot, we go way
beyond Jean-Claude Van Damme's

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.270
Kickboxer.
And I think a lot of that'll

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:01.510
become clear in the other excerpt.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:02.830
90s references, yeah.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.973
Let's jump into some specifics here.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:11.330
This boy, who, again,
you'll have to discover

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:15.363
later who that is, if
it's one of us or not,

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.630
when he goes into the manhole at the end,

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:22.670
it's like I have a specific
vision, like I know

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:26.823
the specific manhole because
we did this, you know?

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:30.453
If you leave your house and
you go around the corner,

00:13:31.396 --> 00:13:34.090
there's, of course there
were, there are sewage lines

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:37.620
and manholes everywhere, and we might

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.620
have jumped into more
than one, but you know

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:41.910
one particular one I'm thinking about,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:46.670
but across the street from,
you go around Adam Nicholson's,

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:48.690
well you go down the
street from Adam Nichols

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.120
and it's more like where
Justin lived, Justin McCloud.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:52.953
Yeah.

00:13:52.953 --> 00:13:54.237
And then you go down into the woods.

00:13:54.237 --> 00:13:55.530
Headed towards Lake Small
and then you get into, yeah.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:56.810
So you get into the woods and there's like

00:13:56.810 --> 00:14:00.360
these big corridors where
the trees have been removed

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.320
and they put in sewage lines to get

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:06.850
from one part of Lake
Small into Buies Creek.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:10.040
And I assumed that this
exists in other towns

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:13.140
around the world, but it
was definitely something

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.650
that, I mean the interesting thing is that

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.600
it didn't really exist
over near your house

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.250
because there would be like
corridors for power lines,

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:22.083
but there was something,

00:14:22.083 --> 00:14:23.740
because there was a water treatment plant,

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:25.650
I guess there still is
a water treatment plant,

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.370
and it's very close to Keith Hills,

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.600
the country club that I lived outside of,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:34.420
did not live inside of,
but there were these

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.470
corridors cut through the trees,

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:38.610
and it was basically how
we sort of mapped out

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:42.380
the woods that we played
in, and could also,

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:43.840
if you continued following them,

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:45.840
you would get to places, like,

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.760
we talked about this in
The Book of Mythicality

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:51.210
how we followed one of these
for a really long time,

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.410
and some turns, and ended up
at Chris Barefoot's house,

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:54.243
and we were like, what?

00:14:54.243 --> 00:14:56.760
He lives over here, and this is connected,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:59.150
now I'm near I'm near the
Harnett County Airport,

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:00.970
and this is blowing my mind.

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:04.890
But at one time we just
went up to a manhole,

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.660
and we took a stick, or I kind of think we

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.650
had to get a crow bar,
but we eventually learned

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:11.347
how to do it with a stick.

00:15:11.347 --> 00:15:12.180
A lot of times, in fact,

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.470
we didn't put this in the documentary,

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.110
maybe we'll make this like a special scene

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:18.638
for the society or something,

00:15:18.638 --> 00:15:22.370
we opened up a manhole
right next to the river

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.381
using a stick, cause a lot
of times they didn't put them

00:15:25.381 --> 00:15:29.240
in quite right when
they set them back down,

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.316
so you could kind of get a stick in there

00:15:31.316 --> 00:15:32.240
and kind of pry it open.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:35.893
It would be tough for a
kid to do it by himself,

00:15:35.893 --> 00:15:37.633
but you know, this is fiction.

00:15:39.022 --> 00:15:40.250
But if your hand's cut.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:42.070
But if you're running for your life.

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:43.463
Yeah, he's running for his life.

00:15:43.463 --> 00:15:45.010
I bet the adrenaline
could pull that thing off.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:49.460
And so, we pulled off the
lid of a manhole cover

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.480
down in the woods beyond Justin's house,

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.720
and then you crawl down
the ladder to the bottom.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.500
And you just sit there, you smell it.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.060
You smell the waste of all your neighbors.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:05.330
And there's like an accumulation of--

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:06.530
What's old.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.710
Refuse I'll call it.

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:12.240
But it was wild, it was like man,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.720
we're like feeling like a Ninja Turtle.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:17.143
It seemed like a portal to another place.

00:16:17.143 --> 00:16:19.080
I was really trying to
work in a Ninja Turtle

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.250
reference at the end of that,

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.350
but then it didn't happen,
it didn't come together.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.840
But there is Ninja Turtle
reference in the book.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:28.998
That's right, it's just not
right there in the prologue.

00:16:28.998 --> 00:16:32.440
I would recommend going
down into the sewer.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:33.700
No, no, don't do that.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.670
More than twice, I would
recommend doing it once.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.557
Everybody needs to be able
to say they've been into.

00:16:39.557 --> 00:16:44.154
Don't go into sewers
with sores, especially.

00:16:44.154 --> 00:16:48.200
You skipped over a part
that was also an homage

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:49.870
to something that happened to us,

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.480
and that is the story
we've told several times

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.000
about, you know, we would
go to these pastures,

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.500
cow pastures, and we would
run around, chase cows,

00:17:02.013 --> 00:17:05.330
and then there would
typically be like a short

00:17:05.330 --> 00:17:10.090
barbed wired fence that
had a, usually had,

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:13.060
like a line, either in front
of it, or right on top of it,

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.900
which is an electrified fence,

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.040
and of course you'd pee on it,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.860
or you'd do whatever, touch it,

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:18.840
and dare your friends.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.050
I've never peed on it.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:21.910
So you're talking about when
I went over on this thing.

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:24.240
Yeah, but, so the idea of running,

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.720
cause what we did, our
story, in real life,

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:27.930
is running from a bull.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:29.620
Literally when the bull turned on us

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:30.610
and started running after us,

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.868
and me, and you, and Ben ran,

00:17:32.868 --> 00:17:34.465
and me and Ben got over the fence,

00:17:34.465 --> 00:17:36.080
and then of course you,
tried to get over the fence

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.330
and got stuck on the barbed wire fence

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:41.670
and seesawed over it,
and then your leg came up

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.730
and caught the electrified fence

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:46.520
and you had began to get
shocked in a pulsing pattern,

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:47.353
which is how these things work.

00:17:47.353 --> 00:17:48.920
Which was funny to you, but not to me.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.410
Right, you were crying, we were laughing.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.320
I wasn't crying a lot, but I mean.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:54.270
You were crying enough.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.450
Yeah, and actually, in the documentary,

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:57.840
where we went back to Buies Creek,

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:02.400
you can see the field in
Bleak Creek conversations.

