And grounds for that connection,

00:35:28.305 --> 00:35:32.540
which proves to be very
necessary with what they

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:34.240
need to accomplish.
Right.

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:38.514
Well and the whole idea
of putting kidney stones,

00:35:38.514 --> 00:35:43.514
featured in this chapter,
and a lot of kidney stones,

00:35:45.310 --> 00:35:48.313
if you read the book you'll
understand what that's about.

00:35:49.334 --> 00:35:52.380
But, and this was actually
sort of a central point,

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.640
it was one of the, as we were
piecing together the story,

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:57.190
and kind of coming up with
what exactly is going on

00:35:58.250 --> 00:36:02.020
in Bleak Creek, kidney stones is actually

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:04.200
was kind of a starting point.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:06.830
So it's interesting that it's
a starting point for Jeanine

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:08.510
and what she's trying to accomplish.

00:36:08.510 --> 00:36:09.920
Now you've never had a kidney stone?

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:10.753
I haven't.

00:36:10.753 --> 00:36:11.717
I've never had a kidney stone.

00:36:11.717 --> 00:36:13.603
But I am fascinated,

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:18.123
I'm fascinated by and
obsessed with them because I,

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:24.242
you know, I feel like I
have a tendency to get,

00:36:24.242 --> 00:36:25.310
and this might just be in my mind,

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:28.600
I feel like I get weird,
I get weird things,

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:30.970
like you know I've got herniated disks,

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:35.370
and I've got psoriasis,
and I had that thing happen

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:37.230
with my eye, and you know,

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:40.040
I was a chronic nose bleeder growing up,

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:43.730
and I'm a pretty, I'm
not like a sickly person,

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:47.560
but I feel like I have
these, you know, weird,

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:50.380
not life threatening,
but annoying maladies.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.530
So you feel primed to get a kidney stone,

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:55.266
but you think yours will be
shaped like a ninja star?

00:36:55.266 --> 00:36:58.670
Well not that, my brother has had them.

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:00.390
Ninja star stones?

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:01.620
He's had kidney stones.

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:04.420
And, you know, they say.

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:05.480
They'll bring you to your knees, man.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:07.120
The conventional wisdom
of the kidney stone

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:09.090
is that it is the only
thing that is comparable

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:12.790
to a woman giving birth
that a man can experience.

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:14.120
My brother can attest to that,

00:37:14.120 --> 00:37:16.050
basically it's hell on Earth.

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:20.150
It's hell, and it's not
really, you always think,

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:24.840
oh this must hurt when it's
coming out your ding a ling,

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:29.010
and that's not, that's
not the part that hurts.

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:31.210
Coming out your ding a
ling is the fun part.

00:37:33.153 --> 00:37:33.986
Really?

00:37:34.974 --> 00:37:39.974
It's when it begins, it gets
dislodged from your kidney.

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:40.933
Yeah.

00:37:40.933 --> 00:37:43.790
When it basically moves and begin to work

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:45.970
it's way down to your ding a ling.

00:37:45.970 --> 00:37:47.433
Through the urethra as we said.

00:37:47.433 --> 00:37:49.957
No, the urethra I think
is from the bladder.

00:37:49.957 --> 00:37:50.790
Oh.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:51.623
It's gotta get to the bladder.

00:37:51.623 --> 00:37:53.050
So it's the process from getting from the

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:54.860
kidney to the bladder, which, there's,

00:37:54.860 --> 00:37:56.610
I'm sure there's a name for that too.

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:58.860
It's not the urethra as far as I know.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:01.760
And that process, and what most men,

00:38:01.760 --> 00:38:04.810
or women who have this
happen to them, think,

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:09.550
is oh, I pulled a muscle, I'm
having a really bad back day,

00:38:09.550 --> 00:38:11.880
and because I've got
lower back issues already,

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:15.050
every time I feel something,
and it's a little bit different

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:17.950
I'm just like oh no, I'm
gonna get kidney stones now

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:18.783
because Cole got them,

00:38:18.783 --> 00:38:20.900
and it's just a matter of
time before I get them.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:24.860
My dad had one and it was so big,

00:38:24.860 --> 00:38:28.210
cause they say, well the best
thing to do is just to pass it

00:38:28.210 --> 00:38:29.570
and they want you to catch it so they

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:31.783
can analyze it for some reason.

00:38:31.783 --> 00:38:34.270
I think that's also fun as well.

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:37.670
Yeah they, I guess you
get a special basket.

00:38:37.670 --> 00:38:39.760
They give you a lollipop
if you take a kidney stone

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:41.163
to the doctor's office.

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:44.980
But it's kidney stone flavored.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:46.440
Don't get your hopes up.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.960
But that depends on what you ate.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:50.920
If somebody's ate a lot of lollipops.

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:53.138
A lot of tea, you drank a lot of tea.

00:38:53.138 --> 00:38:55.380
Kidney stones taste like tea.

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:57.180
I mean you could open up that jar

00:38:57.180 --> 00:39:00.540
and taste on of Big Gary's I'm
sure when he wasn't looking.

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:03.170
Sprinkle them on your
pizza like that red stuff

00:39:03.170 --> 00:39:04.470
you put on pizza at the table.

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:07.360
I think they're just made of calcium.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:11.160
My dad's was so big
that they had to go in,

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:15.830
go up in there, and then they
grabbed it and crushed it.

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:17.258
Ouch.

00:39:17.258 --> 00:39:19.810
And then my nanny, I
remember when I was a kid,

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:22.730
she went in and she came
out from a procedure,

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:26.430
and she had a T-shirt and it
said I've been lithotripsed.

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:27.510
Lithotripsy-ed.

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:28.740
That's a T-shirt you can get?

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:33.740
Lithotripsy is when they blast
sound waves at your kidney

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:36.610
and break up a kidney stone

00:39:36.610 --> 00:39:38.480
to make it smaller so it can pass.

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:41.410
And for some reason only certain stones

00:39:41.410 --> 00:39:42.470
are candidates for that.

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:44.240
Like my brother, I don't
think has had that done,

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:45.490
I think he's just had to pass them.

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:47.420
He's had a lot though.

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:49.690
He's, I don't know, maybe
three, four, I don't know.

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:52.050
Big Gary's had a jar full of them.

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:54.530
And there is, so as we
were trying to figure out

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:57.700
like how many kidney stones are too many

00:39:57.700 --> 00:40:01.120
because it unfolds why
there's kidney stones

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:03.181
and why people are getting them,

00:40:03.181 --> 00:40:04.783
why Big Gary has them,
and why there are so many,

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:09.890
the world record for
kidney stones is actually,

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:14.880
there's somebody who passed,
I don't remember what it was,

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:17.650
the numbers of kidney stones
that we put into the book

00:40:17.650 --> 00:40:21.500
were based on looking up what's
a reasonable amount here,

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:23.710
what's like a maximum
amount of kidney stones.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:24.543
In a year?

00:40:24.543 --> 00:40:26.030
It's not a ridiculous amount.

00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:29.310
There are some people who
can form them that quickly,

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:30.900
depending on the conditions.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:32.280
That's gotta be a nightmare.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:34.560
I don't wanna ever experience that.

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:35.740
Let's read another excerpt.

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:38.020
But Little Dino's was an actual place.

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:38.853
Yeah.

00:40:38.853 --> 00:40:41.496
In Buies Creek, it was the
only restaurant in town,

00:40:41.496 --> 00:40:43.980
it wasn't that great.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:46.200
I actually don't remember
ever eating there.

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:47.560
We also didn't spend a lot of time,

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:50.430
we didn't like spend money on food.

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.200
You know, we ate at home,

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:55.190
and it wasn't like hey mom give
me $5 I wanna go get pizza.

00:40:55.190 --> 00:40:56.240
Is it my turn to read an excerpt?

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:57.130
It is, yeah.

00:40:57.130 --> 00:40:59.930
Rex now turned into a gravel driveway.

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:03.360
The small wheels of his scooter
grinding to a crunchy stop

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:06.960
on the loose rocks that lead
to the house of Travis Bathoon.

00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:10.710
Not only was Travis the
nicest person Rex and Lead

00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:14.270
had ever met, seemingly
never disparaging anyone,

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:17.040
but he had an untold number of jobs,

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:20.960
landscaper, septic tank
pumper, chimney sweep,

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:25.510
house painter, and most
important, volunteer fireman.

00:41:25.510 --> 00:41:28.510
He traveled from job to job
on his bright red moped,

00:41:28.510 --> 00:41:30.840
and wore a thick black leather belt

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:34.700
that held a walkie talkie,
two beepers, a flashlight,

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:37.610
a large bowie knife, and a giant keyring

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:40.233
loaded with what looked
to be 30 keys or more.

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:44.810
The teenagers around Bleak
Creek regularly referred to him

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:47.240
as redneck Batman.

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:49.170
He didn't seem to mind the moniker,

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.840
and Rex and Leaf didn't
know if that was due

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:53.760
to his unwavering positivity or just the

00:41:53.760 --> 00:41:57.530
general allure of being
compared to Batman.

00:41:57.530 --> 00:42:00.170
Suffice it to say, when
Rex realized he needed

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:03.560
a fire extinguisher, he
immediately thought of Travis,

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:07.020
who called Rex back three
minutes after he beeped him.

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:10.410
Rex walked the rest of the way
up to the single wide trailer

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:14.160
which Travis referred to
as his ranch style house,

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:15.880
despite the wheels under the mobile home

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:19.560
being only somewhat obscured
by the wood lattice skirting.

00:42:19.560 --> 00:42:21.050
Travis opened the door before Rex

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:23.210
even reached the front steps.

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:27.890
Hey man, he said, flashing his
signature grin, come on in,

00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:30.570
my casa, your casa.

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:33.990
Rex didn't often see Travis
without his utility belt,

00:42:33.990 --> 00:42:36.010
he seemed smaller.

00:42:36.010 --> 00:42:39.160
Thanks Travis, Rex said, hit
with the smell of solder wire

00:42:39.160 --> 00:42:42.710
and bacon grease as he stepped
onto the brown shag carpet.

00:42:42.710 --> 00:42:45.540
He'd only been to Travis's
house once before,

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:47.417
as he always seemed to be out and about.

00:42:47.417 --> 00:42:50.130
Glad you're here, Travis said,

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:52.180
adopting a serious tone, he added,

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:55.140
you know, I ain't supposed to
share the tools of the trade

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:57.080
with a layman like yourself,

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:00.020
but I'm willing to make
an exception for you.

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:01.630
Now what do you need it for again?

00:43:01.630 --> 00:43:04.105
For Polter Dog, Travis asked.

00:43:04.105 --> 00:43:09.105
No, a new project, it's
kind of experimental.

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:12.460
You mean like about
chemistry or something?

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:14.440
Rex felt horrible lying to Travis,

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:17.690
but it was just too easy and necessary.

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:20.090
Sort of, yeah.

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:24.850
You boys are so dang
creative, a chemistry movie.

00:43:24.850 --> 00:43:26.960
That sounds awesome.

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:28.370
Yep.

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:29.440
You sure you don't want me to come

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:32.460
and keep an eye on things
when you do the pyro?

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:33.650
Travis asked.

00:43:33.650 --> 00:43:35.330
I live for that kind of stuff.

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:37.150
He leaned in and spoke in a whisper.

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:40.780
To tell you the truth, that's
why I'm a volunteer fireman.

00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:42.920
I love to watch stuff burn.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.760
Catching himself, he said loudly.

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.893
Of course, I love putting it out too.

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:51.220
I don't think you'd find
this too interesting,

00:43:51.220 --> 00:43:54.040
just sparklers, Rex said,
adding another thread

00:43:54.040 --> 00:43:56.230
to his web of white lies.

00:43:56.230 --> 00:43:59.650
Hm, yeah, that ain't really even fire.

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.853
Travis paused, looking at
the fake wood paneled wall,

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:05.100
all right, lemme get it.

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:10.130
Travis got up and walked
through a bead curtain

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:11.670
to the back of his trailer.

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:14.410
Rex stood waiting, surveying
Travis's assortment

00:44:14.410 --> 00:44:18.550
of what looked to be about 100
California raisins figurines

00:44:18.550 --> 00:44:20.500
on the kitchen counter.

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:22.200
There were lots of repeats,

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:24.283
at least 20 of the one on the skateboard.

00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:27.370
They're cool as hell, huh?

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:29.150
Travis said, returning with an ancient

00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:31.070
looking fire extinguisher.

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:33.920
Yeah, pretty cool Rex said.

00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:36.300
Smartest thing Hardy's ever did.

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:39.540
I wish they'd bring them back,
he said, shaking his head.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.420
Anyway, here she is, he extended the

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:44.113
fire extinguisher to Rex.

00:44:44.113 --> 00:44:48.130
Thanks Travis, I really
appreciate this Rex said.

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:49.313
My pleasure buddy.

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:55.090
Travis Bathoon is one of
my favorite supporting

00:44:55.090 --> 00:44:56.280
characters in the cast.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:58.540
He's the type of guy that we feel like

00:44:58.540 --> 00:45:02.140
we've known forever, it's
like a very Harnett County

00:45:02.140 --> 00:45:05.500
type guy, it wasn't based
on one particular person.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:06.690
There's a lot of people like this,

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:09.090
there's a lot of people who just have

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:12.280
a lot of equipment on a belt.

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:13.420
On their person at all times.

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:14.770
And do a lot of different things,

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:17.830
and you can't point to
the one job that they do

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:20.620
cause they do a multitude of jobs.

00:45:20.620 --> 00:45:24.420
So he's kind of a composite
character in that way,

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:28.206
and also I do think that the reference to

00:45:28.206 --> 00:45:32.830
thinking of your trailer as a ranch home,

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:35.027
I didn't ever hear anyone say that,

00:45:35.027 --> 00:45:38.053
but that struck me as a very funny thing.

00:45:38.053 --> 00:45:40.170
Something they could have thought.

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:43.050
Well because, you know, there's different

00:45:43.050 --> 00:45:45.910
styles of trailers,
there's different levels.

00:45:45.910 --> 00:45:46.743
Sure Rhett.

00:45:46.743 --> 00:45:49.300
You got some trailers that are just like

00:45:49.300 --> 00:45:52.460
I'm not trying to disguise,
like it's obvious,

00:45:52.460 --> 00:45:54.400
like we don't have any skirting on this,

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:55.700
we could hook a truck up to it

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:56.740
and immediately get out of here

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:57.573
if we needed to.

00:45:57.573 --> 00:46:01.120
Yeah, but some people, they
don't unhook the truck.

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:04.610
But then there are the
ones that are established,

00:46:04.610 --> 00:46:06.440
the wheels haven't moved in years.

00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:09.500
They've planted like a small
garden around the trailer,

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:14.200
and you begin to be lulled
into the impression that

00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:16.970
this is just somebody's ranch style home.

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:19.251
There might be a foundation under there.

00:46:19.251 --> 00:46:20.210
I think the thing about Travis is,

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:22.193
is fixation on things burning.

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:25.010
That was based on a true story.

00:46:25.010 --> 00:46:27.810
Or maybe not, maybe don't
wanna be too incriminating.

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:30.330
We'll we're not, I'm not
gonna say who it was,

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:34.560
but we were told, there was
rumor that went around town,

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:38.450
and I believe he was at some
point prosecuted for this,

00:46:38.450 --> 00:46:40.430
but there was a rumor that
a person who was on the

00:46:40.430 --> 00:46:42.360
volunteer fire department.

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:44.810
Was always the first to
the scene of the fire.

00:46:44.810 --> 00:46:47.580
And it turns out he was
setting homes on fire,

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:51.483
but not occupied homes,
like abandoned homes.

00:46:51.483 --> 00:46:52.880
Abandoned homes, they go up quick.

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:54.620
Like how'd he get here so quick,

00:46:54.620 --> 00:46:59.610
like, my ex step dad Jimmy
was also a volunteer fireman.

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:01.900
And my next door neighbor
Pete Dinklage was.

00:47:01.900 --> 00:47:03.300
And in the middle of church,

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:05.580
sometimes, like, the pastor
would be giving a sermon,

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:08.240
and then you could hear across town,

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:11.000
the siren go off for
the volunteer firehouse.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:13.030
They didn't use beepers,
they just had a giant.

00:47:13.030 --> 00:47:14.070
He actually had a beeper too.

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:17.100
But they use a giant
siren that can be heard

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:19.430
throughout the entire town of Buies Creek.

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:21.870
And if the preacher would be preaching,

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:23.550
and then all of the
sudden Jimmy would get up

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:27.770
and run out of the back of the sanctuary,

00:47:27.770 --> 00:47:29.360
and I thought that was cool.

